# `actuarial::Mortality::qx`

[Back to `actuarial::Mortality`](../Mortality.md)

The one-year death probabilities, the form the law is stored in.

## Signature

```cpp
const pluss::Series &actuarial::Mortality::qx() const noexcept;
```

## What It Does

Returns {math}`q_{x+k}` for `k = 0, ..., n−1`: the probability of dying in year `k + 1` given alive at its start. This is the stored representation, so the accessor costs nothing and returns a reference; every other view is computed from it. The reference lives as long as the law, and for an undefined law it is an undefined `Series`.

## Returns

A const reference to a one-dimensional `Series` of `horizon` probabilities.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1: reading the probabilities

The values a law was built from.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const pluss::Series qx = pluss::make_series({0.010, 0.012, 0.015, 0.019, 0.024});
  const actuarial::Mortality mortality = actuarial::mortality::from_qx(qx);
  out.writeln(mortality.qx().items<double>());
}
```

### Example 2: from another representation

A law built from forces stores the corresponding probabilities, {math}`q=1-e^{-\mu}`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const actuarial::Mortality m = actuarial::mortality::from_force(pluss::make_series({0.01, 0.02, 0.03}));
  out.writeln("qx =", m.qx().items<double>(), " 1 - exp(-0.02) =", 1.0 - std::exp(-0.02));
}
```

### Example 3: probabilities of a parametric law

The Gompertz law gives increasing probabilities with age.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const actuarial::Mortality m = actuarial::mortality::gompertz(pluss::make_series({60.0, 70.0, 80.0, 90.0}), 0.0001, 1.08);
  out.writeln(m.qx().items<double>());
}
```

### Example 4: an undefined law

The accessor does not throw; it returns an undefined series.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  out.writeln("qx defined for a default law:", actuarial::Mortality{}.qx().defined());
}
```

